{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc | sed -n '52,100p;126,205p;916,955p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc | sed -n '110,203p;825,880p;925,948p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md | sed -n '190,215p'; nl -ba public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog | sed -n '36,55p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc | sed -n '52,100p;126,205p;916,955p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc | sed -n '110,203p;825,880p;925,948p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md | sed -n '190,215p'; nl -ba public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog | sed -n '36,55p'\"","aggregated_output":"    52\t\n    53\t;; =============================================================================\n    54\t;; D0: the published dimension and its hash domain (§4, datom.md §META-PROTOCOL)\n    55\t;; =============================================================================\n    56\t\n    57\t(def contract-version\n    58\t  \"The :yin.debruijn/* contract version. Version 1 is type-preserving:\n    59\t   :int64 and :double are disjoint, and integral doubles are never\n    60\t   folded into :int64.\"\n    61\t  1)\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\t(def canonical-value-table\n    65\t  \"§5's canonical value table as data: the classes a projected scalar may\n    66\t   carry and the rule that fixes each one's encoding. Bounds are symbolic\n    67\t   (:signed-int64, :ieee-754-safe-integer) so the table — and therefore\n    68\t   the descriptor that embeds it — prints identically on every host;\n    69\t   `canonical-class` below pins the numbers. D3's encoder consumes this\n    70\t   table; D0 consumes it for validation.\"\n    71\t  {:classes [:nil :bool :int64 :double :string :bytes :keyword :symbol\n    72\t             :map :set :vector :list],\n    73\t   :framing {:slots :tagged-and-length-delimited},\n    74\t   :nil {:encoding :own-tag},\n    75\t   :bool {:bytes {false \"00\", true \"01\"}},\n    76\t   :int64 {:domain :signed-int64, :endianness :little},\n    77\t   :double {:format :ieee-754,\n    78\t            :nan :one-quiet-nan,\n    79\t            :signed-zero :distinct,\n    80\t            :integral-double-folding false},\n    81\t   :string {:encoding :utf-8, :normalization :nfc, :framing :length-prefixed},\n    82\t   :bytes {:framing :length-prefixed},\n    83\t   :keyword {:components [:namespace :name], :encoding :nfc-utf-8},\n    84\t   :symbol {:components [:namespace :name], :encoding :nfc-utf-8},\n    85\t   :map {:key-order :canonical-encoded-bytes},\n    86\t   :set {:element-order :canonical-encoded-bytes},\n    87\t   ;; vectors and lists are distinct classes: `vector?` observes the\n    88\t   ;; difference, so one class would be an undeclared semantic collision\n    89\t   :vector {:order :positional},\n    90\t   :list {:order :positional},\n    91\t   :numbers {:javascript {:int64 :safe-integer-only,\n    92\t                          :unsafe-integer :diagnostic},\n    93\t             :out-of-domain [:bigint :ratio :char :other-numeric]},\n    94\t   :limits [:nfc-normalization-collisions]})\n    95\t\n    96\t\n    97\t(def dimension-slots\n    98\t  \"The projected d5 semantic slots in §4's declared order, each as\n    99\t   [slot-name slot-type slot-role]. Roles: :scalar enters node identity;\n   100\t   :child is an ordered child ref carrying child hashes; :marker is\n   126\t\n   127\t\n   128\t(def descriptor\n   129\t  \"The :yin.debruijn/* dimension published per datom.md's meta-protocol:\n   130\t   the small d3 subgraph [s a v] declaring arity, ordered slots, the\n   131\t   canonical encoding rule parameterized by the slot types, and the\n   132\t   anchor morphisms. The content hash of this subgraph — `dimension-hash`\n   133\t   below — IS the dimension's identity and the projection's hash domain\n   134\t   separator: not an ad hoc text string.\"\n   135\t  [[:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/contract-version contract-version]\n   136\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/arity 22]\n   137\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/slots dimension-slots]\n   138\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\n   139\t    :dim/encoding\n   140\t    {:hash :sha256,\n   141\t     :node-hash \"SHA-256(dimension-hash || encode(tag-specific-slots-in-descriptor-order))\",\n   142\t     :slot-order :descriptor,\n   143\t     :tag-specific-slots-only true,\n   144\t     :hash-excluded-slots [:yin.debruijn/hash :yin.debruijn/root],\n   145\t     :child-order :declared,\n   146\t     :values canonical-value-table}]\n   147\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\n   148\t    :dim/projection-to\n   149\t    [[:d1 \"content-addressing floor: every projected node reduces to its node hash\"]\n   150\t     [:d3 \"semantic floor: each projected record is (h, :yin.debruijn/<slot>, v)\"]]]\n   151\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\n   152\t    :dim/lift-from\n   153\t    [[:d5 \"the named :yin/* Universal AST datoms (yin.vm/ast->datoms-with-root)\"\n   154\t      \"yin.vm.debruijn/project-datoms is the lift\"]]]])\n   155\t\n   156\t\n   157\t;; dimension-hash is minted below, beside the canonical encoder: §5's\n   158\t;; domain separator is the descriptor encoded through the settled encoder\n   159\t;; and hashed, and the encoder's definitions intervene between the two.\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t;; =============================================================================\n   163\t;; D0: the canonical value table as validation, and the NFC seam (§5)\n   164\t;; =============================================================================\n   165\t\n   166\t(defn- bytes-like?\n   167\t  [v]\n   168\t  #?(:clj (bytes? v)\n   169\t     :cljs (instance? js/Uint8Array v)\n   170\t     :cljd (instance? Uint8List v)))\n   171\t\n   172\t\n   173\t#?(:cljs\n   174\t   (defn- js-number-class\n   175\t     \"The JS adapter's classification of a JS number, whose one type cannot\n   176\t      tell 1 from 1.0 (a host limit that stays here, never in the universal\n   177\t      projection): a safe integer is :int64; NaN, the infinities, -0.0, and\n   178\t      non-integral values are :double; an unsafe integral is nil, a\n   179\t      diagnostic rather than a silently misclaimed int64.\"\n   180\t     [v]\n   181\t     (cond\n   182\t       (not= v v) :double\n   183\t       (or (= js/Infinity v) (= js/-Infinity v)) :double\n   184\t       (and (zero? v) (= js/-Infinity (/ 1.0 v))) :double\n   185\t       (not= v (js/Math.floor v)) :double\n   186\t       (<= -9007199254740991 v 9007199254740991) :int64\n   187\t       :else nil)))\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t(defn- numeric-class\n   191\t  \"Classify a number under the type-preserving numeric rule (contract\n   192\t   version 1): int64 and double are disjoint. Host integers (Long on the\n   193\t   JVM, Dart int) are :int64 and every floating-point value is :double,\n   194\t   integral or not; bigints, ratios, and friends classify nil. Only the\n   195\t   :cljs adapter, where 1 and 1.0 are one number, classifies by value.\"\n   196\t  [v]\n   197\t  #?(:clj (cond\n   198\t            (int? v) :int64\n   199\t            (float? v) :double\n   200\t            :else nil)\n   201\t     :cljs (js-number-class v)\n   202\t     :cljd (if (int? v) :int64 :double)))\n   203\t\n   204\t\n   205\t(defn- code-unit-at\n   916\t    :else (encode-value v)))\n   917\t\n   918\t\n   919\t(def dimension-hash\n   920\t  \"The published hash domain separator (§4): the descriptor data encoded\n   921\t   through the settled canonical encoder and hashed, per datom.md's 'the\n   922\t   content hash of this subgraph IS the dimension's identity'. D0-D2\n   923\t   minted a transitional dao.jing digest; D3 re-pins it here over the\n   924\t   settled byte rules. The descriptor content is unchanged — only the\n   925\t   digest's derivation moved to the canonical encoder, so the separator\n   926\t   now separates this dimension from every other by the same bytes every\n   927\t   node hash uses.\"\n   928\t  (jing/sha256 (encode-value descriptor)))\n   929\t\n   930\t\n   931\t(defn canonical-value\n   932\t  \"A record's stored scalar is its canonical spelling (the carried D3\n   933\t   obligation): the long form for integers and the double form for\n   934\t   doubles, so 1 and 1.0 hold distinct record contents; the NFC form for\n   935\t   strings and ident parts, and the same recursively inside collections.\n   936\t   One content address, one record content: equal fingerprints imply equal\n   937\t   :records maps.\n   938\t   Canonicalizing a map whose keys merge — {1 :a, 1.0 :b}, or two string\n   939\t   keys with one NFC form — is a key collision, diagnosed rather than\n   940\t   letting iteration order decide which entry survives; a set whose\n   941\t   elements merge — #{1 1.0}, or two spellings of é — is diagnosed the\n   942\t   same way, because the merge changes its count, not just a scalar's\n   943\t   spelling.\"\n   944\t  [v]\n   945\t  (let [class (canonical-class v)]\n   946\t    (case class\n   947\t      :nil v\n   948\t      :bool v\n   949\t      :int64 (long v)\n   950\t      :double (double v)\n   951\t      :string (normalize-nfc v)\n   952\t      :bytes v\n   953\t      :keyword (if-let [ns (namespace v)]\n   954\t                 (keyword (normalize-nfc ns) (normalize-nfc (name v)))\n   955\t                 (keyword (normalize-nfc (name v))))\n   110\t    (is (= 22 (count (:dim/slots dimension))))\n   111\t    (is (= [:yin.debruijn/hash :yin.debruijn/type :yin.debruijn/arity\n   112\t            :yin.debruijn/bound :yin.debruijn/free :yin.debruijn/value\n   113\t            :yin.debruijn/op :yin.debruijn/key :yin.debruijn/prefix\n   114\t            :yin.debruijn/buffer :yin.debruijn/parked-id :yin.debruijn/macro?\n   115\t            :yin.debruijn/body :yin.debruijn/operator\n   116\t            :yin.debruijn/operands :yin.debruijn/test :yin.debruijn/consequent\n   117\t            :yin.debruijn/alternate :yin.debruijn/target :yin.debruijn/val-node\n   118\t            :yin.debruijn/source :yin.debruijn/root]\n   119\t           (mapv first (:dim/slots dimension)))\n   120\t        \"the slots are declared in §4's order\")\n   121\t    (is (= [:ordered-vector :ref]\n   122\t           (nth (some #(when (= :yin.debruijn/operands (first %)) %)\n   123\t                      (:dim/slots dimension))\n   124\t                1))\n   125\t        \"the ordered-vector slot type is declared\")\n   126\t    (is (= :sha256 (get-in dimension [:dim/encoding :hash])))\n   127\t    (is (= [:yin.debruijn/hash :yin.debruijn/root]\n   128\t           (get-in dimension [:dim/encoding :hash-excluded-slots])))\n   129\t    (is (= d/canonical-value-table (get-in dimension [:dim/encoding :values])))\n   130\t    (is (= #{:d1 :d3} (set (map first (:dim/projection-to dimension)))))\n   131\t    (is (= :d5 (ffirst (:dim/lift-from dimension))))\n   132\t    (is (= (jing/sha256 (d/encode-value d/descriptor)) d/dimension-hash)\n   133\t        \"the domain separator is the descriptor encoded through the settled\n   134\t         canonical encoder and hashed — D3's re-pin of datom.md's dimension\n   135\t         identity, not an ad hoc string\")\n   136\t    (is (= \"90a5235794c9eac968490d343633e30c68d1a96093125df7ad1ac3c7398a43d4\"\n   137\t           d/dimension-hash)\n   138\t        \"the settled digest, pinned: descriptor drift fails here as a\n   139\t         descriptor failure, not as a definition restating itself\")\n   140\t    (is (re-matches #\"^[0-9a-f]{64}$\" d/dimension-hash))))\n   141\t\n   142\t\n   143\t;; =============================================================================\n   144\t;; D0: the canonical value table as data and validation (§5)\n   145\t;; =============================================================================\n   146\t\n   147\t(deftest canonical-classes-are-the-tables\n   148\t  (are [x expected] (= expected (d/canonical-class x))\n   149\t    nil :nil\n   150\t    true :bool\n   151\t    \"s\" :string\n   152\t    :ns/k :keyword\n   153\t    'sym :symbol\n   154\t    7 :int64\n   155\t    #?@(:cljs [] :default [7.0 :double])\n   156\t    1.5 :double\n   157\t    (/ 0.0 0.0) :double\n   158\t    (/ 1.0 0.0) :double\n   159\t    (/ -1.0 0.0) :double\n   160\t    -0.0 :double\n   161\t    #?@(:cljs [0.0 :int64] :default [0.0 :double])\n   162\t    #?@(:cljs [] :default [9.3e18 :double])\n   163\t    [1 2.5] :vector\n   164\t    (list 1 2.5) :list\n   165\t    {:a 1} :map\n   166\t    #{1} :set\n   167\t    [nil \"x\" :k] :vector\n   168\t    {:a [1.0 #{2}]} :map\n   169\t    [(fn [x] x)] nil\n   170\t    {\"k\" (fn [x] x)} nil)\n   171\t  (is (d/canonical-value? [1 {:a #{2}}]))\n   172\t  (is (not (d/canonical-value? {:a (fn [x] x)}))))\n   173\t\n   174\t\n   175\t(deftest jvm-out-of-domain-values-diagnose\n   176\t  ;; §5's out-of-domain numerics, asserted where the host can express them\n   177\t  #?(:clj (are [x] (nil? (d/canonical-class x))\n   178\t            5N\n   179\t            (bigint 9223372036854775808)\n   180\t            1/2\n   181\t            (java.math.BigDecimal. \"1.1\")\n   182\t            \\a\n   183\t            [1 \\c])))\n   184\t\n   185\t\n   186\t(deftest a-javascript-number-is-int64-only-when-safe\n   187\t  ;; an unsafe integer's exact classification is not recoverable (§5)\n   188\t  #?(:cljs (do\n   189\t             (is (= :int64 (d/canonical-class 9007199254740991)))\n   190\t             (is (nil? (d/canonical-class 9007199254740992)))\n   191\t             (is (nil? (d/canonical-class -9007199254740992)))\n   192\t             (is (nil? (d/canonical-class 9.3e18)))\n   193\t             (is (nil? (d/canonical-class 9223372036854775808)))\n   194\t             (is (= :unsupported-value\n   195\t                    (:rule (throws-data #(d/encode-value 9007199254740992)))))\n   196\t             (is (= \"0200000010ffffffffffff1f00\" (d/encode-value 9007199254740991))))))\n   197\t\n   198\t\n   199\t(deftest the-nfc-seam-composes-on-every-host\n   200\t  (let [decomposed \"e\\u0301\", composed \"\\u00e9\"]\n   201\t    (is (= composed (d/normalize-nfc decomposed)))\n   202\t    (is (= composed (d/normalize-nfc composed)))\n   203\t    (is (= \"abc\" (d/normalize-nfc \"abc\")))))\n   825\t           (:rule (throws-data\n   826\t                    #(d/datoms->projected\n   827\t                       (mapv (fn [x]\n   828\t                               (if (= :yin.debruijn/hash (nth x 1))\n   829\t                                 [(nth x 0) (nth x 1) 42 (nth x 3) (nth x 4)]\n   830\t                                 x))\n   831\t                             ds)))))\n   832\t        \"a non-string hash value diagnoses rather than throwing a host\n   833\t         regex error\")))\n   834\t\n   835\t\n   836\t(deftest integral-doubles-and-integers-are-distinct\n   837\t  ;; a JS number cannot tell 1 from 1.0; that host limit stays in the JS\n   838\t  ;; adapter, so only the JVM and Dart lanes assert the distinction\n   839\t  (when #?(:cljs false :default true)\n   840\t    (let [int-version (project (lam '[x] (app (v '+) (v 'x) (lit 1))))\n   841\t          double-version (project (lam '[x] (app (v '+) (v 'x) (lit 1.0))))]\n   842\t      (is (not= (:fingerprint int-version) (:fingerprint double-version))\n   843\t          \"contract version 1: int64 and double are disjoint classes\")\n   844\t      (is (not= (:records int-version) (:records double-version))))))\n   845\t\n   846\t\n   847\t;; =============================================================================\n   848\t;; D3: the settled byte rules, pinned (§5, §8's cross-host rows)\n   849\t;; =============================================================================\n   850\t;; Every literal below is asserted by the JVM, Node, and Dart lanes alike:\n   851\t;; one pinned byte string, three hosts. If any host's encoder diverges by one\n   852\t;; bit, its lane fails here — that is the cross-host byte-identity proof, and\n   853\t;; the essay fingerprint below is the stable §8 row. (A live pair transport —\n   854\t;; one Dart peer spawned by the JVM/Node lanes, per the R5 precedent — needs\n   855\t;; files outside this dispatch's box and is flagged to the orchestrator.)\n   856\t\n   857\t(deftest the-settled-scalar-bytes-are-pinned\n   858\t  (are [expected v] (= expected (d/encode-value v))\n   859\t    \"02000000100100000000000000\" 1\n   860\t    #?@(:cljs []\n   861\t        :default [\"0300000010000000000000f03f\" 1.0])\n   862\t    \"0200000010ffffffffffffffff\" -1\n   863\t    \"0200000010ffffffffffff1f00\" 9007199254740991\n   864\t    \"0300000010000000000000f87f\" (/ 0.0 0.0)\n   865\t    \"03000000100000000000000080\" -0.0\n   866\t    \"0300000010000000000000f83f\" 1.5\n   867\t    \"0300000010000000000000f07f\" (/ 1.0 0.0)\n   868\t    \"0400000003abc\" \"abc\"\n   869\t    \"0400000002é\" \"é\"\n   870\t    \"0400000003€\" \"€\"\n   871\t    \"0400000004😀\" \"😀\"\n   872\t    \"060000001a0400000002op0400000004echo\" :op/echo\n   873\t    \"070000001800000000000400000004list\" 'list\n   874\t    \"0000000000\" nil\n   875\t    \"010000000201\" true\n   876\t    \"010000000200\" false)\n   877\t  ;; the full int64 extremes exist only where a 64-bit integer does: a JS\n   878\t  ;; number cannot hold them exactly — §5's JS-number rule — so the boundary\n   879\t  ;; row every host shares is 2^53-1 above\n   880\t  (when #?(:cljs false :default true)\n   925\t       encoding, declared in §5\")\n   926\t  (is (= (d/encode-value {:a 1, :b 2}) (d/encode-value {:b 2, :a 1})))\n   927\t  (is (= (d/encode-value #{1 2}) (d/encode-value (conj #{2} 1)))\n   928\t      \"maps and sets sort by encoded parts, so iteration order never\n   929\t       enters the bytes\"))\n   930\t\n   931\t\n   932\t(deftest the-essays-fingerprint-is-stable\n   933\t  (is (= \"dca760e0b5e2416fbb8e88f41539f6bdcb8708c19079e26f5cbec3fd1c93c3ad\"\n   934\t         (:fingerprint (project worked-example))\n   935\t         (:fingerprint (d/project-datoms\n   936\t                         (vec (reverse (datoms-of worked-example)))))\n   937\t         (:fingerprint (d/project-datoms\n   938\t                         (vec (concat (drop 3 (datoms-of worked-example))\n   939\t                                      (take 3 (datoms-of worked-example)))))))\n   940\t      \"the essay's (fn [count] (+ count 1)): one pinned fingerprint for the\n   941\t       renamed binder and shuffled datom input alike\"))\n   942\t\n   943\t\n   944\t(deftest records-carry-canonical-scalars\n   945\t  (let [int-version (project (lam '[x] (app (v '+) (v 'x) (lit 1))))\n   946\t        double-version (project (lam '[x] (app (v '+) (v 'x) (lit 1.0))))\n   947\t        literal-value (fn [p]\n   948\t                        (->> (:records p) vals\n   190\tso iteration order never decides a hash. The preimage is a text stream: each\n   191\tpart frames as its class's tag byte, an 8-hex-digit length, and the content —\n   192\tnumeric and byte content is hex, string content is the raw UTF-8 text, and\n   193\tthe length counts the content's UTF-8 bytes. The hashed bytes are the\n   194\tstream's UTF-8 encoding.\n   195\t\n   196\tNumeric canonicalisation (Contract Version 1, `:yin.debruijn/contract-version`\n   197\t1, `:integral-double-folding false`): the classes `:int64` and `:double` are\n   198\tdisjoint. Exact integers in signed int64 use int64 encoding; every\n   199\tfloating-point value, integral or not, uses IEEE-754 double encoding and is\n   200\tnever folded into `:int64`. All NaNs use one quiet-NaN encoding and `+0` and\n   201\t`-0` remain distinct. Consequently integer `1` and double `1.0` project to\n   202\tdistinct records and fingerprints, and `{1 :a, 1.0 :b}` and `#{1 1.0}` do not\n   203\tcollide. An earlier draft folded integral doubles into int64; the\n   204\tunreleased contract never shipped that rule.\n   205\t\n   206\tThe boundary: JavaScript has one number type, so a JS host cannot tell `1`\n   207\tfrom `1.0`. That is a host quirk, and it stays in the JS adapter: on CLJS a\n   208\tsafe integer classifies as `:int64`, a non-integral number as `:double`, and\n   209\tan unsafe integral is diagnostic. It does not contaminate the universal\n   210\tprojection, whose bytes are type-preserving on JVM and Dart. A foreign record\n   211\tclaiming a class the host cannot represent is refused at the boundary with\n   212\t`:unsupported-value`. Bigints, ratios, characters, and other out-of-domain\n   213\tnumeric objects are diagnostic. NFC normalisation collisions are an inherited\n   214\tlimit of the repository encoding.\n   215\tThe Dart NFC source is settled 2026-09-21: the pure-Dart `unorm_dart`\n    36\t                   [:h2 \"3. One Truth, Many Interpretations\"]\n    37\t                   [:p [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" is built on an architectural axiom: \" [:em \"Interpretation Creates Semantics (one truth, many perspectives).\"] \" Rather than one monolithic representation, it keeps one source of truth and derives other forms from it, each with its own identity or none:\"]\n    38\t                   [:ul\n    39\t                    [:li [:strong \"The Named Universal AST (implemented): \"] \"the canonical stored, queryable, and renderable form, with the exact names the developer wrote. It has no single hash. The AST walker evaluates it directly, and the semantic VM executes a separately linearized \" [:code \":yin.code/*\"] \" image lowered from it.\"]\n    40\t                    [:li\n    41\t                     [:strong\n    42\t                      \"The De Bruijn projection and its fingerprint \"\n    43\t                      \"(implemented, dormant on the sharing path): \"]\n    44\t                     \"an alpha-canonical Merkle encoding of the named AST. \"\n    45\t                     \"Node hashes are hash-consed over a published \"\n    46\t                     \"dimension, transaction and provenance never enter \"\n    47\t                     \"the preimage, scalars are NFC-canonicalized, and the \"\n    48\t                     \"bytes are pinned identical on JVM, JavaScript, and \"\n    49\t                     \"Dart. Its records are stored as datoms. Contract \"\n    50\t                     \"Version 1 preserves numeric type: \"\n    51\t                     [:code \"1.0\"] \" and \" [:code \"1\"]\n    52\t                     \" project to distinct records. It still drops tail \"\n    53\t                     \"flags, which makes it a good identity for \"\n    54\t                     \"alpha-equivalence and unusable as an executable \"\n    55\t                     \"form. This is the closest thing yin.vm has to \"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
